  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1027 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nVALLEY RAIN AND MOUNTAIN SNOW CONTINUE:\n\nCurrent satellite imagery indicates that the center of the low \npressure that has brought widespread mountain snow and valley rain \nis currently located over the Central Divide mountains, continuing \nto track northeastward. Moisture wrapping around the backside of \nthis low is keeping valley rain and higher elevation snow showers \ngoing across much of central- and north-western Colorado this \nevening. This has resulted in sloppy road conditions over mountain \npasses, so be cautious and prepared for winter driving if out \ntravelling tonight. The low is expected to reach the eastern \nColorado Plains by daybreak Thursday, with the plume of wrap-around \nmoisture finally moving east of the Divide. An additional 1-3 inches \nof snow will be possible across the central and northern Divide \nranges, with a trace to an inch for mainly the northern San Juans. \nSome higher snow amounts are possible along the Uncompahgre River \nGorge south of Ouray, as this area tends to fare better than \nguidance indicates under these types of northerly flow, wrap-around \nmoisture events. Winter Weather Advisories remain in effect for \nelevations above 9500 feet in the central and northern Divide \nmountains through 6AM Thursday morning. \n\nDRYING OUT THROUGH THE WEEKEND:\n\nAs the low tracks east into the plains, energy in the base of the \nparent trough splits off and begins to retrograde into Southern \nCalifornia. Between these two features, high pressure noses in from \nthe west, bringing drier, quieter weather to eastern Utah and \nwestern Colorado. By Friday, the retrograded area of low pressure \nremains stationary across SoCal, with high pressure to the north, \ncreating a Rex Block for the West, while deep troughing dominates \nthe eastern CONUS. This will keep conditions stagnant for several \ndays, with the next chance for active weather across eastern Utah \nand western Colorado not expected until early to mid-next week. \nModels remain in poor agreement over most details regarding this \nnext system, so stay tuned. In the meantime, clear skies and a much \ndrier airmass are expected for the Western Slope. Temperatures will \nstart out 5-10 degrees below normal, but gradually warm to near \nnormal by the weekend. Clearing skies and this much colder airmass \nin place will promote effective radiational cooling Thursday night \ninto Friday morning, especially across northwest Colorado and into \nsome of the higher elevation valleys in the central-western and \nsouthwestern Colorado. A Freeze Watch is in effect for these areas \nfrom late Thursday night through late Friday morning. Widespread \ntemperatures of 25-30F are expected, with values dropping as low as \n20F in favored spots. If you have sensitive vegetation or outdoor \nplumbing, now is a good time to take the necessary precautions to \nprotect them.",
